WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.090
He's currently earning raves for his work
as melancholic mob boss Russell Bufalino in

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.710
Martin Scorsese's Netflix epic The Irishman,
but it seems the end of the road could be

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.720
near for Joe Pesci's acting career.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.820
Here's why The Irishman might be Pesci's final
film.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.050
To be clear, there's been no official news
or confirmation about Pesci's retirement,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.980
and there may not be, as Pesci hasn't exactly
been one to offer career updates to the press

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:24.760
over the years.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.360
But the actor has entered semi-retirement
before.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.679
Since 1998's Lethal Weapon 4, Pesci has appeared
in just four feature films: 2006's The Good

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.000
Shepherd, 2010's Love Ranch, 2015's A Warrior's
Tail, and this year's The Irishman.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.960
Pesci has made a staunch habit of saying "no"
to the movie business over the past couple

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:44.800
of decades.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:45.480
"No."

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.060
But it seems he'll at least consider answering
the call if he has a chance to work with trusted

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.740
friends like "Marty" and "Bob."

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.379
He likely wouldn't have even appeared in two
of his last four movies, The Good Shepherd

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:59.840
and The Irishman, if not for the incessant
urging of his long-time pal Robert De Niro.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.210
It was De Niro, after all, who coaxed Pesci
back in front of the camera after an eight-year

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:05.910
hiatus for The Good Shepherd.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.320
Pesci's other good friend, revered director
Martin Scorsese, saw what the actor did with

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.610
so little in that film.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.500
And thus, the director, who previously worked
with Pesci and De Niro in Raging Bull, Goodfellas,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.140
and Casino, was clearly eager to get some
of that low-key Pesci goodness on camera in

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:21.780
The Irishman.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.280
But even when Marty and Bob call him up, it's
far from a given that Pesci will jump back

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.300
in front of the camera.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.540
Scorsese and De Niro apparently had to ask
Pesci between 40 and 50 times before he signed

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.160
on to appear as Russell Buffalino in The Irishman.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.600
If you're wondering why Pesci finally agreed
to star in The Irishman, Scorsese said in

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.751
a recent interview with Entertainment Weekly
a handful of reasons that may or may not have

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.300
influenced Pesci's decision:

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.320
"It's not even about the money or about being
compensated and appreciated for your value.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.980
It's about the physicality of [making a film]
where nobody's giving you anything.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.960
At a certain age and physicality for the actors,
it may not be worth it."

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.690
Like Pesci himself, his old movie crew is
also getting up there in age.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.820
Though Scorsese and De Niro continue to crank
out movies at an impressive pace, there's

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.580
little doubt that eventually even they will
have to slow down – which means that there

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.010
will likely be fewer and fewer chances for
Pesci to even think about boarding a film

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.260
project moving forward.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.160
While Pesci maintained a remarkably low profile
in the movie business over the last 20 years,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.860
he's been keeping himself quite busy in other
areas.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.540
During Pesci's first sabbatical from acting
in the early 2000s, he spent much of his time

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.960
making his way into the so-called "sport of
kings" horse racing.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.640
Pesci has always taken the sport quite seriously,
claiming that he backed into being an owner

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.709
and breeder because he enjoyed horse racing
so much.

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:41.840
For the record, he still does, and remains
a mainstay on the scene.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.960
Though he's had successes and failures in
both realms, Pesci would likely claim neither

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.360
acting nor horse racing as his true passion.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.060
That title will seemingly always belong to
music, with Pesci first stepping foot into

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.400
the entertainment business as a singer way
back in the '60s.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.940
Pesci was actually pals with singers Frankie
Valli and Tommy DeVito when he was growing

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.280
up in Belleville, New Jersey, and he introduced
the pair to songwriter Bob Gaudio.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.260
Yes, that means Pesci is at least partly responsible
for the formation of the iconic vocal group

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.300
The Four Seasons.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.879
As Pesci watched his pals rise to superstardom,
he made a go of it in the music biz himself,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.240
releasing his first solo album, Little Joe
Sure Can Sing!, in 1968.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.660
The album, and his music career as a whole,
was a moderate success at best, with Pesci

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.220
eventually finding more acclaim as an actor
nearly a decade later.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.640
Still, Pesci has returned to the recording
booth often since then.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:38.790
He put out a second album in 1998, Vincent
LaGuardia Gambini Sings Just For You, which

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:43.000
was, of course, released under the pseudonym
of his iconic My Cousin Vinny character.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.360
"Did you say yutes?"

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.840
"Yeah, two yutes."

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.460
"What is a yute?"

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.220
"Oh, excuse me, Your Honor.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.660
Two youths."

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:57.540
In November 2019, Pesci released a new album
of jazz-tinged crooners called Still Singing,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.530
which finds him belting out standards alongside
the likes of Adam Levine and jazz icon "Little"

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:03.280
Jimmy Scott.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.360
Slate recently named Still Singing one of
the Best Jazz Albums of 2019.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.329
It's a distinction incredible enough to possibly
convince Pesci to keep his music career going

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:15.300
for the foreseeable future, and shy away from
acting again.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.160
Whether it was money, the relentless nature
of filmmaking, or a simple desire to take

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.160
it easy in his late 70s that was holding Pesci
back from getting back into acting, his contributions

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.600
to The Irishman simply cannot be underplayed.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.220
Many critics agree that the film is nothing
if not a first-rate Scorsese picture, featuring

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.540
the first truly great De Niro performance
in who knows how many years, along with equally

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.860
brilliant contributions from Al Pacino, Harvey
Keitel, Ray Romano, and Anna Paquin.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.220
But the fact is that Pesci steals the show
with his marvelously understated work as a

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.810
quietly domineering mobster.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.180
"He has no choice.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.900
He has no choice.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:52.800
These are the higher ups."

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.410
However Scorsese and De Niro finally convinced
Pesci to appear in The Irishman, they were

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.640
indeed rewarded for their efforts with a career-crowning
performance.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.460
If The Irishman truly does become Pesci's
final film, he would certainly go out on top.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.250
As much as we'd like to see the actor carry
on in front of the camera for years to come,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.310
we really can't help but feel like The Irishman
isn't just a pitch-perfect swan song for one

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.380
of the greats of the acting game, but also
a well-earned one.

